# Git workflow

## Commits

Follow **[Conventional Commits 1.0.0](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/)**:

- Use types such as `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`.
- Optional scope: `feat(search): …`
- Breaking changes: footer `BREAKING CHANGE:` or `!` after the type, per the spec.
- Name the ticket at the end of the description: `feat(i18n): format dates per locale (AXM-093)`.
  It has to be readable in `git log --oneline`, which is where someone asks what a change was for.
  It goes at the end because commitlint's `subject-case` rule rejects a subject that opens with an
  uppercase token, and that rule is worth more than the position of the reference.

One commit per ticket. A pull request may carry several, as long as each one stands on its own.

## Merging

Merge a pull request with a merge commit (`gh pr merge <number> --merge --auto --delete-branch`).
Squashing loses the ticket boundaries inside the branch, and rebasing loses the link to the pull
request; a merge commit keeps both.

## Branches

Use one of these prefixes for every branch (no other top-level prefixes):

- **`feature/`** — new work, improvements, refactors tied to normal delivery.
- **`hotfix/`** — urgent production fixes.

Examples: `feature/playlist-grid`, `hotfix/audio-player-seek`.

## Secrets

Do not commit secrets. Use `.env.dist` as the checklist for required environment variables.
